# Pickman Street butcher shop.
# Otto Kessler's widow: tradesman anger, unearned shame, and the domestic echo of the cult.
NPC: widow_kessler
LOCATION: pickman_street
SCHEDULE: morning=kessler_shop, midday=kessler_shop, evening=kessler_parlor, night=kessler_parlor

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(widow_kessler, default) AND coat = plain
  TAG: kessler_plain
  MRS. KESSLER: "The shop is shut. The ice went yesterday."
  [She looks past Walter's shoulder toward the street before meeting his eyes.]
  MRS. KESSLER: "You're not wearing the brass. That's something, at least.\n\nCome inside before the flies find the counter."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(widow_kessler, default)
  TAG: kessler_badge
  MRS. KESSLER: "Captain Odell already steered me down the steps once this morning, Officer."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I'm not Captain Odell."
  MRS. KESSLER: "You wear the same tin on your pocket.\n\nIf you're here to ask what Otto paid to sit with those gentlemen, look at the books yourself. I never saw a penny of it come back."

TOPIC: the_knife
  GATE: evidence(knife)
  TAG: kessler_knife
  LABEL: "Ask about Otto's boning knife"
  TIME: 4
  WALTER CORWIN: "I found a boning knife beneath a hedge root. Wiped clean.\nMaker's stamp matches the blades on your rack."
  MRS. KESSLER: "Otto's good knife. Carbon steel. Kept it in grease under the counter."
  WALTER CORWIN: "It hadn't touched meat."
  MRS. KESSLER: "Not hog meat. Not beef.\n\nHe wrapped it in oilcloth three weeks back and put it in his breast pocket like a fountain pen. A butcher carrying his trade into an evening room. He thought that made him their equal."
  NOTEBOOK: kessler_knife_1 | "Mrs. Kessler confirms the boning knife belonged to Otto. He carried it to the Ophion estate three weeks ago, wrapped in oilcloth."
  EVIDENCE: kessler_knife_confirmed

TOPIC: club_standing
  GATE: always
  TAG: kessler_standing
  LABEL: "Ask how Otto came to join the club"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "Judge Wexford, Dr. Fenn, Corliss, Pruitt. Men of property.\nHow did Kessler join them?"
  MRS. KESSLER: "They needed someone who didn't flinch at gristle."
  [She wipes an already scrubbed butcher block with a dry rag.]
  MRS. KESSLER: "Wexford called him 'tradesman' to his face. Corliss wouldn't take a cigar from his case.\nBut on the last Thursday of the month, a carriage came to the alley behind this shop. Not the front door. Never the front."
  WALTER CORWIN: "And he went willingly?"
  MRS. KESSLER: "He wore his wedding suit. God forgive him, he thought they were inviting him into the county."
  NOTEBOOK: kessler_standing_1 | "Otto Kessler was excluded socially by the club's elite members, but collected via carriage at his rear shop entrance every last Thursday."
  EVIDENCE: kessler_carriages

TOPIC: butcher_parcels
  GATE: evidence(kessler_carriages)
  TAG: kessler_parcels
  LABEL: "Ask what Otto brought to the meetings"
  TIME: 6
  FORK:
    CHOICE: "Ask about meat deliveries."
      MRS. KESSLER: "It wasn't beef he carried in butcher paper. We hadn't slaughtered that morning."
      WALTER CORWIN: "What was it?"
      MRS. KESSLER: "Salt. Coarse gray curing salt from the harbor sheds, three pounds at a time, pressed into cakes.\nHe said the kitchen cellar required it dry."
      NOTEBOOK: kessler_salt_parcels | "Kessler carried parcels of coarse gray harbor salt to the estate meetings, ostensibly for the kitchen cellar."
      EVIDENCE: gray_salt_cakes
    CHOICE: "Ask about what he carried away."
      MRS. KESSLER: "He came back with empty pockets and dirt under his nails."
      WALTER CORWIN: "Earth?"
      MRS. KESSLER: "Wet sand. Sea silt. It smelled like the low-tide flats behind the old try-works.\nI washed his cuffs myself. You don't get that stink from river mud."
      NOTEBOOK: kessler_silt_cuffs | "Kessler returned from meetings with sea silt and low-tide try-works stench beneath his nails and cuffs."
      EVIDENCE: tryworks_silt

TOPIC: the_mother
  GATE: evidence(kessler_knife_confirmed) AND NOT topic_done(widow_kessler, the_mother)
  TAG: kessler_mother
  LABEL: "Ask how Otto spoke about the club at the end"
  TIME: 5
  MRS. KESSLER: "Toward the end, he stopped bragging about the dinners."
  WALTER CORWIN: "What did he speak about?"
  MRS. KESSLER: "'She'll have us home,' he told me. Right there by the stove, taking off his boots."
  [Her voice drops, thin and unsteady.]
  MRS. KESSLER: "'She's no different from any mother,' he said. As if he were comforting a child with a fever.\nI asked him whose mother. He looked at me like he couldn't remember where he'd learned the word."
  NOTEBOOK: kessler_mother_1 | "Kessler spoke of a maternal power toward the end: 'She'll have us home... She's no different from any mother.'"
  EVIDENCE: maternal_delusion

TOPIC: coat_difference
  GATE: coat = plain AND (evidence(kessler_carriages) OR topic_done(widow_kessler, club_standing)) AND NOT topic_done(widow_kessler, coat_difference)
  TAG: kessler_coat
  LABEL: "Ask why she told Walter instead of Odell"
  MRS. KESSLER: "Odell looked at my shop and saw six paragraphs for the Gazette.\nYou came in a coat that smells like damp wool and rain, same as Otto's work coat.\n\nA woman knows when a man is looking for a corpse instead of an excuse."
